WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.312
Welcome!

00:00:01.312 --> 00:00:04.332
In this Java tutorial, you'll write generic

00:00:04.332 --> 00:00:07.422
classes and interfaces: reusable definitions

00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:10.722
that keep input, stored-value, and result types

00:00:10.722 --> 00:00:14.023
consistent when callers choose different types.

00:00:14.663 --> 00:00:17.485
Applications often need the same storage

00:00:17.485 --> 00:00:20.236
operations for different kinds of data.

00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:23.411
Generics let you reuse those operations while

00:00:23.411 --> 00:00:26.797
the compiler checks whether each caller supplies

00:00:26.797 --> 00:00:28.913
and receives compatible types.

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:32.663
Today we'll help campus workshop staff keep two

00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.503
separate collections: participant names and

00:00:35.503 --> 00:00:37.154
whole-number quiz scores.

00:00:37.154 --> 00:00:39.664
We'll store Maya and Luis in the names

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:42.570
collection, and four and seven points in the

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.759
scores collection.

00:00:44.404 --> 00:00:47.274
One Shelf class will support both collections

00:00:47.274 --> 00:00:49.571
through a shared Sequence interface.

00:00:49.571 --> 00:00:52.250
We'll report the first name, the number of

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.163
names, and the combined score.

00:00:54.163 --> 00:00:57.161
Then you'll predict the reports for a different

00:00:57.161 --> 00:00:58.054
set of inputs.

00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:01.617
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:02.292
Main.java.

00:01:06.096 --> 00:01:07.324
The file is open.

00:01:07.324 --> 00:01:10.504
Both collections need to add a value, read a

00:01:10.504 --> 00:01:13.250
value by index, and report their size.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.502
Let's import the familiar ArrayList class and

00:01:16.502 --> 00:01:19.898
describe those operations in a generic Sequence

00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:20.621
interface.

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:32.344
T is a type parameter: a name for a type used

00:01:32.344 --> 00:01:34.999
consistently within this definition.

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:37.729
Add accepts a T, and get returns a T.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:41.122
Size returns an int because an item count does

00:01:41.122 --> 00:01:43.409
not depend on the element type.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:46.434
T is not a stored value and is not a Java

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:47.024
keyword.

00:01:47.034 --> 00:01:50.390
The interface states the operations, but it does

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.928
not store our entries.

00:01:51.928 --> 00:01:54.654
Let's define a generic Shelf class that

00:01:54.654 --> 00:01:57.801
implements Sequence and keeps its values in a

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:59.059
private ArrayList.

00:02:05.282 --> 00:02:07.584
Shelf declares its own type parameter.

00:02:07.584 --> 00:02:10.431
In implements Sequence, that parameter supplies

00:02:10.431 --> 00:02:12.249
the interface's type argument.

00:02:12.249 --> 00:02:14.732
The private list uses the same parameter,

00:02:14.732 --> 00:02:17.640
connecting the promised operations to compatible

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.125
storage.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:20.912
Choosing String for a Shelf use connects those

00:02:20.912 --> 00:02:23.638
source-level types to String; it does not put

00:02:23.638 --> 00:02:26.182
the word String into the list as an entry.

00:02:26.194 --> 00:02:29.212
Each shelf needs an initially empty list.

00:02:29.212 --> 00:02:32.597
Let's create that list in the constructor so a

00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:35.394
new shelf starts with its own storage.

00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:44.033
This constructor creates an ArrayList and stores

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.890
its reference in this shelf's private field.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.006
Calling the constructor again creates a separate

00:02:50.006 --> 00:02:51.759
shelf with a separate list.

00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:54.355
The two collections will share the class

00:02:54.355 --> 00:02:57.017
definition without sharing their entries.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.651
Now we can supply the first interface operation.

00:03:00.651 --> 00:03:04.271
Let's implement add by passing its value to this

00:03:04.271 --> 00:03:05.855
shelf's private list.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:15.494
The parameter and stored elements both use T, so

00:03:15.494 --> 00:03:18.544
the operation and the list agree about their

00:03:18.544 --> 00:03:18.890
type.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.079
Add changes the list by appending an entry; it

00:03:22.079 --> 00:03:23.742
returns no result value.

00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:26.792
The Override annotation asks the compiler to

00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:29.981
check that this method implements the intended

00:03:29.981 --> 00:03:31.367
interface operation.

00:03:31.391 --> 00:03:34.667
Our caller also needs an indexed value and a

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:35.113
count.

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:38.687
Let's implement get and size by requesting those

00:03:38.687 --> 00:03:41.516
operations from the same private list.

00:03:53.722 --> 00:03:56.965
Get returns the element at the requested index,

00:03:56.965 --> 00:04:00.071
using the familiar zero-based list positions.

00:04:00.071 --> 00:04:02.486
Size returns the number of entries.

00:04:02.486 --> 00:04:04.556
Neither call removes an entry.

00:04:04.556 --> 00:04:07.869
We'll use indexes that exist in our example; the

00:04:07.869 --> 00:04:10.767
generic type relationship does not make an

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:12.147
invalid index valid.

00:04:12.166 --> 00:04:13.527
The class is ready.

00:04:13.527 --> 00:04:16.392
Main contains the main method where this

00:04:16.392 --> 00:04:18.255
standalone example starts.

00:04:18.255 --> 00:04:21.693
Let's create a Shelf for String values, refer to

00:04:21.693 --> 00:04:24.773
it through Sequence of String, and add Maya

00:04:24.773 --> 00:04:25.991
followed by Luis.

00:04:40.063 --> 00:04:43.290
String is the type argument here, while Maya and

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.239
Luis are the values we store.

00:04:45.239 --> 00:04:48.130
The declared interface type makes the three

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.087
promised operations available to the caller.

00:04:51.087 --> 00:04:54.045
Names now has Maya at index zero and Luis at

00:04:54.045 --> 00:04:54.717
index one.

00:04:54.717 --> 00:04:57.675
Let's print the first name and the number of

00:04:57.675 --> 00:04:58.213
entries.

00:05:06.598 --> 00:05:08.772
The names reports are in place.

00:05:08.772 --> 00:05:11.857
We need the same operations for whole-number

00:05:11.857 --> 00:05:15.083
scores, so let's create a separate Shelf using

00:05:15.083 --> 00:05:17.748
Integer and add four and seven points.

00:05:27.731 --> 00:05:30.626
Generic type arguments use reference types,

00:05:30.626 --> 00:05:33.455
which is why this declaration uses Integer

00:05:33.455 --> 00:05:34.532
rather than int.

00:05:34.532 --> 00:05:37.764
Java boxes each supplied whole-number value into

00:05:37.764 --> 00:05:40.457
the wrapper form used by the collection.

00:05:40.457 --> 00:05:43.690
These entries belong to the scores shelf; adding

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.181
them does not change the names shelf.

00:05:46.214 --> 00:05:49.296
To report the combined score, let's read both

00:05:49.296 --> 00:05:52.514
entries, add their values, and print the total.

00:06:01.158 --> 00:06:04.019
The two get calls return Integer values.

00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:07.310
Java unboxes them for addition, and the result

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.314
goes into the separate int variable total.

00:06:10.314 --> 00:06:13.676
Reading the values does not replace either list

00:06:13.676 --> 00:06:15.036
entry with the sum.

00:06:15.036 --> 00:06:18.183
Let's click Run and check all three reports.

00:06:23.306 --> 00:06:26.178
First: Maya comes from index zero of names.

00:06:26.178 --> 00:06:29.116
Names: two confirms that its two entries are

00:06:29.116 --> 00:06:29.583
stored.

00:06:29.583 --> 00:06:32.722
Total: eleven comes from four points plus seven

00:06:32.722 --> 00:06:35.460
points in the separate scores collection.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.265
We used one class definition, two distinct

00:06:38.265 --> 00:06:41.403
objects, and the same interface operations with

00:06:41.403 --> 00:06:43.006
different element types.

00:06:43.071 --> 00:06:45.624
These type relationships help reject an

00:06:45.624 --> 00:06:48.636
incompatible operation, such as supplying text

00:06:48.636 --> 00:06:50.207
to the Integer sequence.

00:06:50.207 --> 00:06:53.219
They do not tell us whether a numeric score is

00:06:53.219 --> 00:06:54.660
the score we intended.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.802
To check that part of the task, we still compare

00:06:57.802 --> 00:07:00.618
the program's results with known inputs and

00:07:00.618 --> 00:07:01.796
expected behavior.

00:07:02.524 --> 00:07:05.442
We have explained the original reports.

00:07:05.442 --> 00:07:08.810
Let's keep the class and interface unchanged,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:12.402
then use Nora and Eli as the names and three and

00:07:12.402 --> 00:07:13.749
six as the scores.

00:07:25.021 --> 00:07:28.190
Before running, predict the first name, the name

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.973
count, and the total score.

00:07:29.973 --> 00:07:33.010
Trace which object each call reaches and which

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:34.133
indexes it reads.

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:37.236
Also consider whether calculating total changes

00:07:37.236 --> 00:07:38.557
either stored score.

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:41.396
Pause here to work through your prediction.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.442
Let's click Run and compare the reports with

00:07:52.442 --> 00:07:53.515
your prediction.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.505
First: Nora is the entry at index zero.

00:08:01.505 --> 00:08:04.696
Names: two reflects the two names we added.

00:08:04.696 --> 00:08:07.516
Total: nine comes from three plus six.

00:08:07.516 --> 00:08:10.930
The score entries remain three and six because

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:14.048
get reads them and the addition stores its

00:08:14.048 --> 00:08:15.235
result in total.

00:08:15.973 --> 00:08:18.662
We wrote a generic interface and class that keep

00:08:18.662 --> 00:08:21.184
their operation and storage types consistent.

00:08:21.184 --> 00:08:23.593
Each caller chose a type argument, and each

00:08:23.593 --> 00:08:25.050
shelf kept its own values.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.739
The compiler checks compatible types; our result

00:08:27.739 --> 00:08:29.812
checks confirm the intended behavior.

00:08:29.812 --> 00:08:32.389
For another application, think of two kinds of

00:08:32.389 --> 00:08:34.910
data that could use these same operations and

00:08:34.910 --> 00:08:37.488
one wrong value that type checking alone would

00:08:37.488 --> 00:08:38.048
not catch.
